The problem I am having is with the [PexMethod] PUT_IndexOfContract where it is saying 'System.IndexOutOfRangeException : Index was outside the bounds of the array.'. Is there some other attribute that I can use to help PEX? For example instead of [PexMethod], I tried using [PexAllInstancesMethod] but it did not work. 
The point is, it should not throw IndexOutOfBoundException because PEX should be testing different scenarios, right? And IndexOf method should normally return -1 if the item is not found, but it should not throw an exception because it's not an exceptional case in the context of your method.
If I understand correctly there should be a way to tell PEX to handle such situations. Any hint or suggestion would be greatly appreciated.  
In addition, please let me know if you need more info from me to help you understand the issue.

A: This is the kind of behavior you would expect when testing with Pex. Pex aims to explore all possible scenarios/paths, including those that may lead to exceptions. But, since IndexOf method doesn't cause any exception in the normal operation, Pex treats it as a typical case to explore.
So, in this case, Pex would be in overfit since it's covering the paths that aren't necessary in this method.
In case you want to ensure only valid scenario to be tested and not include cases that may lead to exceptions, then you need to constraint your input more. A way to avoid this limitation is to do an assumption over the methods that may raise exceptions.
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
   AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x)); // This would ensure that the list contains the item x

   // Rest of the test...
}

However, please note that above assumption still may not be enough, because Pex internally also does some assumptions to find potential valid inputs, so in some edge cases, it might get incorrect inputs.
For more control you might have to use PexMembers and PexAllMembers attributes to restrict the possible inputs, which can sometimes be resource-intensive.
But again, as a general rule, Pex is just aiming to cover all possible scenarios including those that you might deem as exceptionally unlikely or unlikely paths in your production code.
One more way to handle this is to use PexSettings to tell Pex to ignore exceptions or specific exception types:
[PexSettings("NUnit.Framework.Assertion", Arguments = new object[] { "true" })]

You can also use PexCleanup method to cleanup the data afterwards which may not be necessary for Pex to explore all possible paths. But again that all depends on the context.
For more ways and control over Pex behaviour, you might read the documentation or refer to some existing questions here in SO.

A: Pex will generate as many tests as it can for all the different cases you list, including the ones it deems unlikely or impossible. It's not meant to be a perfect testing tool. The fact that IndexOutOfBoundException is thrown at runtime means it is actually an exceptional case that Pex considers something to test for.
Unfortunately, there's not an easy way to override this behavior for one specific code path without making assumptions about your inputs that are not true in all cases. In your case, as you mentioned, if you want to ensure that IndexOf will only returns -1 when the list does not contain the specified item, you should add an assumption that your list does not contain the item x.
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(x)); // This ensures that the list does not contain the item x

    // Rest of the test...
}

This ensures that the input--------------------        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

    	   // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is non-negative, last index of x in new list == old list count 
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_valueAtIndex = (int)arrList[index];

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_RemovedValueAtIndex = (arrList.Count > Old_index) ? (int)arrList[Old_index] : -1;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_valueAtIndex", Old_valueAtIndex);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_RemovedValueAtIndex", New_RemovedValueAtIndex);

        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_CopyToContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int[] destination, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && destination.Length >= index + arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_destination = (destination.Length >= index) ? destination[index] : -1;

            int[] New_Destination = new int[destination.Length];
            Array.Copy(destination, New_Destination, destination.Length);

            arrList.CopyTo(New_Destination, index);

            int New_DestinationAtOldIndex = (New_Destination.Length >= Old_index) ? New_Destination[Old_index] : -1;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_destination", Old_destination);
            PexObserve.ValueForView--------------------	    // otherwise, we know last index == old index so no issues.
        } 
    }
